A project can expose a public package API to other projects through `@package.moth` at its selected root.

### What the package surface does

`@package.moth` at the project root is the project package's support surface. It exports declarations that external consumers can bind. Without it, the project has no externally consumable package surface, even when `@mod.moth` exists.

The project can also have `@mod.moth`, `@entry.moth` and `config.moth`. The roots share helper files but keep separate declarations and interfaces.

The package surface rejects top-level runtime work, page fragments and builder-slot contributions. It has no entry and emits no route.

### How it reaches descendant modules

The support surface follows the ordinary support visibility rule. It does not receive a privileged namespace over descendant modules, and internal project modules cannot import it as a private back door.

### Privacy and inputs

It cannot depend on `@project` or expose declarations that depend on project-private context. A declaration whose reachable implementation uses private project values cannot leave the project.

Configuration input contracts stay scoped to one project or package boundary. A consumer does not satisfy a dependency's contracts implicitly.

### Registered packages are different

Only an external project package needs a project-root `@package.moth`. Registered Core and Builder source packages can publish a normal `@mod.moth` surface, as `@html` and `@web/site` do.

### Concepts related to the facade basics

- For module root rules see @#module-roots (Module roots).
- For entry runtime and dependency non-activation see @#entry-runtime-and-fragments (Entry runtime and fragments).
